 "The iconic Salt March of 1930 dramatically demonstrated mass defiance by illegally manufacturing salt, directly challenging the deeply resented British monopoly tax.",
 "Nationwide boycotts of British goods, educational institutions, and legal courts effectively paralyzed significant facets of the colonial administrative and economic infrastructure.",
 "These highly visible, morally compelling campaigns successfully captured extensive global media attention, forcing international observers to scrutinize the ethical legitimacy of continued British rule in India."
 ],
 "notes": "Gandhi's methods revolutionized anti-colonial struggle worldwide. Unlike traditional armed rebellion, Satyagraha was designed to expose the moral bankruptcy of the oppressor. By willingly accepting brutal punishment without retaliation (as seen during the Salt March), the protestors forced the British to either back down or appear excessively tyrannical on the world stage. The boycotts were economically damaging, hitting British textile and trade interests hard. This sustained pressure turned what was once an internal colonial matter into a significant international human rights issue, severely damaging Britain's post-war reputation as a champion of democracy."
