 {
 "title": "Foundations of Classical Mechanics: An Introduction to Sir Isaac Newton's Laws of Motion",
 "bullets": [
 "Sir Isaac Newton meticulously formulated three foundational laws that govern the motion of macroscopic objects in the universe.",
 "These universal laws precisely describe the fundamental relationship between the forces acting upon an object and the resulting changes in its state of motion.",
 "Collectively, these three principles establish the bedrock upon which the entire edifice of classical mechanics, or Newtonian physics, is constructed.",
 "Mastery of these concepts is absolutely crucial for accurately analyzing and predicting nearly all observable physical phenomena encountered in our daily environment."
 ],
 "notes": "Welcome to the study of classical mechanics! Today we embark on a journey into the core principles established by Sir Isaac Newton in his seminal work, Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica. These laws, published in the late 17th century, revolutionized our understanding of how things move, moving physics beyond Aristotelian concepts. We will explore how these three elegant statements explain everything from a dropped apple to the orbits of planets. Pay close attention, as these laws form the vocabulary for solving virtually every mechanics problem you will encounter in this course. They represent a monumental achievement in human scientific thought."
 },
 {
 "title": "Newton's First Law: Defining Inertia and the Natural State of Motion",
 "bullets": [
 "An object will perpetually maintain its state of rest or continue moving with constant velocity in a straight line, which is its natural tendency.",
 "This state of unchanging motion persists indefinitely unless an unbalanced, external net force acts upon the object to compel a change.",
 "This inherent resistance an object exhibits toward any alteration in its current state of motion, whether at rest or moving, is precisely defined as inertia.",
 "The amount of matter contained within an object, known as its mass, serves as the direct and quantitative measure of how much inertia that object possesses."
 ],
 "images": [
 {"id": "inertiaexample", "caption": "A book staying still until pushed."}
 ],
 "notes": "Newton's First Law, often called the Law of Inertia, is fundamentally about what happens when the net force ($\sum F$) is zero. If $\sum F = 0$, then the object's acceleration ($a$) must also be zero. This means the velocity ($\vec{v}$) is constant. Crucially, constant velocity includes the case where the velocity is zero (the object is stationary). Inertia is not a force; it is an intrinsic property of matter quantified by mass. A more massive object has more inertia, meaning it is harder to start moving if it is still, and harder to stop or change direction if it is already moving. Think about trying to push a shopping cart versus a full truck—the difference in effort reflects the difference in inertia."
 },
 {
 "title": "Illustrating the Concept of Inertia in Everyday Situations and Motion",
 "bullets": [
 "Automobile seatbelts are engineered safety devices specifically designed to counteract the body's powerful tendency, or inertia, to continue moving forward when the vehicle rapidly decelerates.",
 "During sudden, sharp braking maneuvers, your body attempts to maintain its original forward velocity, causing you to lurch forward relative to the slowing car frame.",
 "Objects that are already in motion possess kinetic inertia, compelling them to maintain their current velocity vector unless an external force intervenes.",
 "Conversely, objects that are stationary possess static inertia, strongly resisting any applied force attempting to initiate movement from that state of rest."
 ],
 "notes": "Inertia manifests dramatically during rapid changes in motion. Consider riding a bus: when the bus starts moving forward abruptly from a stop, your feet move with the bus, but the rest of your body, due to inertia, tends to remain behind at rest, making you feel like you are falling backward. When the bus brakes suddenly, the opposite occurs: your feet stop with the bus, but your upper body wants to keep moving forward at the original speed. These experiences are direct, tangible demonstrations of the First Law. Understanding inertia is key to understanding why we need seatbelts and why maneuvering large objects requires more effort."
 },
 {
 "title": "Newton's Second Law: The Quantitative Relationship Between Force, Mass, and Acceleration ($F{\text{net}} = ma$)",
 "bullets": [
 "The net external force acting upon an object is precisely equal to the product of that object's inertial mass and the resulting acceleration it experiences ($F{\text{net}} = ma$).",
 "For a constant mass, the resulting acceleration experienced by the object is directly and linearly proportional to the magnitude of the net external force applied.",
 "Furthermore, for a constant net force, the acceleration produced is always inversely proportional to the object's inertial mass, meaning more mass yields less acceleration.",
 "The standard international (SI) unit used to quantify force in this equation is the Newton ($\text{N}$), defined as one kilogram-meter per second squared ($\text{kg} \cdot \text{m/s}^2$)."
 ],
 "images": [
 {"id": "fequalsmaformula", "caption": "Diagram showing force, mass, and acceleration relationship."}
 ],
 "notes": "The Second Law is the mathematical heart of Newtonian dynamics. It moves us from qualitative description (Law 1) to quantitative prediction. Remember that $F$ in this equation must represent the net or resultant force—the vector sum of all individual forces acting on the object. If you push a box with $10\text{ N}$ to the right, and friction pushes back with $3\text{ N}$, the net force is $7\text{ N}$ to the right, and this $7\text{ N}$ is what determines the acceleration. This law elegantly connects the cause (force) with the effect (acceleration), mediated by the object's inherent resistance to change (mass). Always check your units to ensure consistency when solving problems."
 },
 {
 "title": "Practical Application and Analysis of Newton's Second Law Calculations ($F=ma$)",
 "bullets": [
 "Students must be able to calculate the resulting acceleration of an object when both the net applied force and the object's mass are accurately known from problem statements.",
 "Conversely, determining the precise magnitude of the net force required to achieve a specified acceleration for an object of known mass is a common calculation.",
 "If the net external force acting upon an object is precisely doubled, assuming mass remains constant, the resulting acceleration must also double proportionally.",
 "Conversely, if the object's mass is doubled while the applied net force remains unchanged, the resulting acceleration will be precisely halved due to increased inertia."
 ],
 "images": [
 {"id": "fequalsmaformula", "caption": "Diagram showing force, mass, and acceleration relationship."}
 ],
 "notes": "When applying $F=ma$, unit consistency is paramount for obtaining the answer in standard units. If force is in Newtons ($\text{N}$), mass must be in kilograms ($\text{kg}$), and the resulting acceleration will be in meters per second squared ($\text{m/s}^2$). A Newton is defined specifically as the force required to accelerate a $1\text{ kg}$ mass at $1\text{ m/s}^2$. For example, if you have a $5\text{ kg}$ object and apply a $20\text{ N}$ force, the acceleration is $a = F/m = 20\text{ N} / 5\text{ kg} = 4\text{ m/s}^2$. Always remember to first calculate the net force by accounting for gravity, tension, friction, and applied pushes/pulls before plugging values into the equation."
 },
 {
 "title": "Newton's Third Law: Understanding Action-Reaction Force Pairs",
 "bullets": [
